    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d656">

                <pb facs="0840" n="832" sameAs="frus:frus1941v04/pg832"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">894.24/7–1941</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Adviser on Political Relations</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Hornbeck</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Under Secretary of State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Welles</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-07-19">July 19, 1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Welles</hi>: There were three parts to the
                    program of action which I suggested in my memorandum of yesterday<anchor n="93"
                        xml:id="d656-f1"/> regarding action to be taken when Japanese action in and
                    against Indochina becomes obvious: namely, economic pressures and,
                    simultaneously with the application thereof, expedition of additional aid to
                    China and new disposals of armed forces (especially planes) in the Far East
                    (especially at Manila). I hope that none of the three will be overlooked and
                    that all three of these measures will be taken simultaneously.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
